ew Error(\"not implemented\"); tests must import them and fail for now.\n- Also write characterisation tests for existing behaviour next to the change that must NOT change; those must pass today. A new app has little of its own: write none when there is nothing to keep.\n- Don't change production code, package.json, tsconfig or the vitest config.\n- You may run \"npx tsc --noEmit\" to check the tests compile. When every test is written, run your own new test files once (npx vitest run <your files>) and read how each test fails: on an assertion or \"not implemented\", never on a crash in the set-up or while rendering. Fix a test that crashes, or that passes, before you return. Run nothing else, and at most twice: the factory runs and judges every test itself in its test lab.\n- Test each criterion at its level: \"unit\" criteria call the module directly; this web app has no API of its own: the API is a separate service with locked tests of its own, and here it is reached only through the generated client. An \"api\" or \"job\" criterion is tested as what this app does with the API's answer: answer the call with the contract's test handlers or a mocked client, then assert what the screen or the web code does. Never test a rule only the server can keep (what is stored, what survives a restart, seeded records, the order a status may change in, what bad data is answered with) by calling the generated client and expecting the rule to hold: nothing in this repo may implement it. Never test the generated client itself. Skip \"manual\" criteria: a person checks those.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store) are never called by a test: the test lab has no network. Put a stand-in behind the seam the code reaches the system through: replace the system's module with vi.mock. The stand-in records every call it gets; assert from that record what was sent (to whom, with which values, how many times), and make the stand-in fail where a criterion is about the system failing. Use a stand-in the tests already have for that system before you write one. A stand-in is test code: keep it with the tests, where it is locked with them.\n- A \"ui\" criterion gets a screen test: a tests/*.test.tsx file whose FIRST line is \"// @vitest-environment jsdom\". Render the screen's container (components/screens/<s-id>/container.tsx, the page with its data and behaviour) with render() from \"@testing-library/react\", and act as a person does with userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\".\n- Assert what a person sees and can do: find things by role and name (screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Save\" })), by label (getByLabelText) or by the words on the page, never by class name, test id or component internals. Use findBy... for what appears after data loads.\n- There is no router, no server and no network in a screen test. Navigation is recorded: import { nav, at } from \"./support/screen\" (from tests/), call at(\"/orders/7\", { tab: \"paid\" }) before render() to set the address, and assert where the page went with nav.went (e.g. expect(nav.went).toContain(\"/orders/7\")).\n- Stand in for the API with vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", ...) or by mocking the generated client module (vi.mock), and answer with data in the shape the contract gives. Give each test its own answers; undo them in afterEach (vi.unstubAllGlobals(), vi.restoreAllMocks()).\n- A screen test proves behaviour on the screen: what is shown for which data, what a click or a typed value does, which message appears. It cannot judge looks (colour, spacing, a width): don't assert styles or class names.\n- Don't edit tests/support/ or the vitest config; the factory owns them. Don't use snapshots.\n\n\n- Give no HTTP probes: the endpoints are served by the API, not by this app. Return an empty list of probes.\nReturn the list of tests you wrote (acId, file, method name).\n\nYou are test writer 1 of 4. The others work at the same time, each on its own copy of this checkout and its own criteria. Yours are: AC-1.1, AC-1.2, AC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-2.3, AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-3.3, AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-5.1. Write tests for these only.\n- Put ALL your tests in ONE new file: acceptance.part1.test.tsx (or .test.ts), in the folder the rules above give for tests. Its name must contain \"part1\".\n- Every helper, fixture or fake you need goes inside that same file, so nothing clashes with another writer's file.\n- Create or change no other file: only that one file is taken from your checkout.\n- You also write the characterisation tests, in that same file.\n\nThe approved-screens section lists the screens a person approved for these requirements: route, states, and the exact words on them (title, buttons, field labels, column headers, empty, error, success and validation messages, toasts; \"change\" for a design note). Where a criterion is about what the user sees or is told, take the expected values from there, word for word, and do not invent other wording. A criterion with no screen there is tested as before. For each test that opens one of the approved screens, also return that screen's id as \"screen\" (the screen the test renders, which can be another than the one its requirement is listed under): the test is then held at the task that builds that screen. Leave it out for a test that opens no screen.\n\nAPI CONTRACT (locked: contracts/openapi.yaml). The app talks to the API only through the generated client in lib/api/client.ts; never write fetch calls or response types by hand, and never edit lib/api or contracts/openapi.yaml. Tests that need the API start the generated handlers from lib/api/client.msw.ts with msw's setupServer (msw/node); they answer with the contract's examples, so assert on that data.","tools":[],"user":"What is already in this checkout for your tests, so you need not search for it:\n\ntests/support/screen.ts (as it is; import from it, don't copy or change it):\n// Written by ai-factory from ai-factory design 20261014-hackathon-problem-statement-fe-ae5c v1 (7e33e7a210ad), approved by Ahsan (via web) on 2026-10-14.\n// What a screen test reads and sets about the address. In tests the app's navigation (lib/nav) is replaced by this:\n// nothing really navigates, and every place the page went to is kept in nav.went.\nexport const nav = { path: \"/\", params: {} as Record<string, string>, went: [] as string[] };\n\n/** Put the test at an address before rendering, e.g. at(\"/orders/7\", { tab: \"paid\" }). */\nexport function at(path: string, params: Record<string, string> = {}): void {\n  nav.path = path;\n  nav.params = params;\n}\n\nexport function resetNav(): void {\n  nav.path = \"/\";\n  nav.params = {};\n  nav.went = [];\n}\n\ntests/support/setup.ts (as it is; import from it, don't copy or change it):\n// Written by ai-factory from ai-factory design 20261014-hackathon-problem-statement-fe-ae5c v1 (7e33e7a210ad), approved by Ahsan (via web) on 2026-10-14.\n// Runs before every test file. A screen test (a *.test.tsx file whose first line is \"// @vitest-environment jsdom\") renders\n// a page with no router and no browser: navigation is recorded in ./screen, and the few browser parts jsdom lacks are\n// filled in. A plain *.test.ts file is not affected.\nimport { afterEach, vi } from \"vitest\";\nimport { resetNav } from \"./screen\";\n\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/nav\", async () => {\n  const { createElement } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  return {\n    useNav: () => ({ path: nav.path, go: (to: string) => { nav.went.push(to); }, param: (name: string) => nav.params[name] ?? null }),\n    Link: ({ to, onClick, ...props }: { to: string; onClick?: (e: unknown) => void } & Record<string, unknown>) =>\n      createElement(\"a\", { ...props, href: to, onClick: (e: { preventDefault(): void }) => { onClick?.(e); e.preventDefault(); nav.went.push(to); } }),\n  };\n});\n\n// A page that uses the router's package itself, not lib/nav, gets the same stand-in: outside the app it has no router.\nvi.mock(\"next/navigation\", async (original) => {\n  const real = await original<typeof import(\"next/navigation\")>();\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  const go = (to: string) => { nav.went.push(to); };\n  return {\n    ...real,\n    useRouter: () => ({ push: go, replace: go, back: () => {}, forward: () => {}, refresh: () => {}, prefetch: () => {} }),\n    usePathname: () => nav.path,\n    useSearchParams: () => new URLSearchParams(nav.params),\n    useParams: () => nav.params,\n  };\n});\n\nvi.mock(\"next/link\", async () => {\n  const { createElement } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  return {\n    default: ({ href, onClick, prefetch: _p, replace: _r, scroll: _s, ...props }: { href: unknown; onClick?: (e: unknown) => void } & Record<string, unknown>) =>\n      createElement(\"a\", { ...props, href: String(href), onClick: (e: { preventDefault(): void }) => { onClick?.(e); e.preventDefault(); nav.went.push(String(href)); } }),\n  };\n});\n\n// A page is tested without the app's frame, so render() puts around it what the frame gives every page: the tooltip\n// provider (a page with a tooltip cannot render without it) and the place toasts appear in.\nvi.mock(\"@testing-library/react\", async (original) => {\n  const real = await original<typeof import(\"@testing-library/react\")>();\n  const { createElement, Fragment } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { TooltipProvider } = await import(\"@/components/ui/tooltip\");\n  const { Toaster } = await import(\"@/components/ui/sonner\");\n  const wrapper = ({ children }: { children: unknown }) => createElement(TooltipProvider, null, createElement(Fragment, null, children as never, createElement(Toaster)));\n  return { ...real, render: (ui: never, options?: object) => real.render(ui, { wrapper: wrapper as never, ...options }) };\n});\n\nif (typeof window !== \"undefined\") {\n  const noop = () => undefined;\n  class Observer { observe = noop; unobserve = noop; disconnect = noop; takeRecords = () => []; }\n  const w = window as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;\n  w.ResizeObserver ??= Observer;\n  w.IntersectionObserver ??= Observer;\n  w.matchMedia ??= (query: string) => ({ matches: false, media: query, onchange: null, addEventListener: noop, removeEventListener: noop, addListener: noop, removeListener: noop, dispatchEvent: () => false });\n  const el = Element.prototype as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;\n  el.scrollIntoView ??= noop;\n  el.hasPointerCapture ??= () => false;\n  el.setPointerCapture ??= noop;\n  el.releasePointerCapture ??= noop;\n}\n\nafterEach(async () => {\n  resetNav();\n  if (typeof document !== \"undefined\") (await import(\"@testing-library/react\")).cleanup();\n});\n\nEach screen's code is components/screens/<screen id in lower case>/container.tsx: s-1, s-2, s-3, s-4, s-5, s-6, s-7, s-8, s-9, s-10, s-11, s-12.\n\nlib/api/client.msw.ts exports these API test handlers and example answers (generated from the contract; use them, don't write your own): getGetOverviewResponseMock, getGetImportBatchesResponseMock, getPreviewImportResponseMock, getSynchronizeImportsResponseMock, getGetLotsResponseMock, getMapPurchaseOrderLineResponseMock, getUpdateLotAssumptionsResponseMock, getGetLotForecastResponseMock, getGetInventoryItemsResponseMock, getRecordInventoryMovementResponseMock, getRecordActualWastageResponseMock, getUpdateInventorySettingsResponseMock, getGetInventoryOutlookResponseMock, getGetRationsResponseMock, getCreateRationResponseMock, getOptimizeRationResponseMock, getSelectRationCandidateResponseMock, getGetFeedingPlansResponseMock, getGenerateFeedingPlanResponseMock, getGetFeedingActivitiesResponseMock, getRecordFeedingActivityResponseMock, getGetWeightReadingsResponseMock, getCreateWeightReadingResponseMock, getUpdateWeightReadingResponseMock, getDeleteWeightReadingResponseMock, getGetLotPerformanceResponseMock, getGetOperationalReportsResponseMock, getGetFeedToGainReportResponseMock, getGetProjectDemoResponseMock, getLoadDemoDataResponseMock, getGetOverviewMockHandler, getGetImportBatchesMockHandler, getPreviewImportMockHandler, getSynchronizeImportsMockHandler, getGetLotsMockHandler, getMapPurchaseOrderLineMockHandler, getUpdateLotAssumptionsMockHandler, getGetLotForecastMockHandler, getGetInventoryItemsMockHandler, getRecordInventoryMovementMockHandler, getRecordActualWastageMockHandler, getUpdateInventorySettingsMockHandler, getGetInventoryOutlookMockHandler, getGetRationsMockHandler, getCreateRationMockHandler, getOptimizeRationMockHandler, getSelectRationCandidateMockHandler, getGetFeedingPlansMockHandler, getGenerateFeedingPlanMockHandler, getGetFeedingActivitiesMockHandler, getRecordFeedingActivityMockHandler, getGetWeightReadingsMockHandler, getCreateWeightReadingMockHandler, getUpdateWeightReadingMockHandler, getDeleteWeightReadingMockHandler, getGetLotPerformanceMockHandler, getGetOperationalReportsMockHandler, getGetFeedToGainReportMockHandler, getGetProjectDemoMockHandler, getLoadDemoDataMockHandler, getFeedlotOperationsAPIMock.\n\n<artifact id=\"acs\" kind=\"acceptance-criteria\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"The mock procurement and receiving source contains purchase-order and receiving records with available field values.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays each returned record's source identifier, vendor, cost, head count, weight, breed, age, source, and row status, as available.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the import preview or synchronization results.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"A synchronization response contains added, duplicate or already-present, and failed row outcomes.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays separate added, duplicate or already-present, and failed row counts, and displays the source row and reported failure reason